"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms=globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms||[]).push([[2052],{4272(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"Paradigms","permalink":"/concepts/tags/paradigms","allTagsPath":"/concepts/tags","count":6,"items":[{"id":"state-space-search-and-optimization/backtracking","title":"Backtracking","description":"Backtracking is an algorithmic paradigm used to solve problems in which the number of possible candidate solutions is very large, but many of those candidates can be discarded early because they violate some constraint. The central idea is simple: construct a solution step by step, and as soon as a partial solution cannot possibly lead to a valid complete solution, abandon it and return to the previous step.","permalink":"/concepts/state-space-search-and-optimization/backtracking"},{"id":"state-space-search-and-optimization/branch-and-bound","title":"Branch and Bound","description":"When solving hard combinatorial problems, Backtracking provides an exact solution by executing a systematic Depth-First Search (DFS). However, it does so blindly, relying entirely on hitting an unfeasible boundary before turning around.","permalink":"/concepts/state-space-search-and-optimization/branch-and-bound"},{"id":"algorithm-design-paradigms/brute-force","title":"Brute Force & Exhaustive Search","description":"When facing a novel computational problem, our immediate priority as engineers is not performance, but correctness. Before we can optimize a solution, we must prove that the problem is solvable.","permalink":"/concepts/algorithm-design-paradigms/brute-force"},{"id":"algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer","title":"Divide and Conquer","description":"Divide and conquer is one of the oldest and most powerful strategies for designing algorithms. Instead of attacking a problem as a whole, it breaks it into smaller problems of the same kind, solves them (usually by applying the same strategy again), and combines their solutions. We have already met two examples: binary search, which discards half of the array at each step, and Quicksort, which partitions the array and sorts each part.","permalink":"/concepts/algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer"},{"id":"algorithm-design-paradigms/dynamic-programming","title":"Dynamic Programming","description":"Divide and conquer breaks a problem into subproblems and combines their solutions. It works beautifully when the subproblems are independent. But we saw in the divide and conquer chapter that the naive recursive Fibonacci solves the same subproblems again and again, and becomes exponential. Dynamic programming keeps the idea of solving a problem through its subproblems, but solves each subproblem only once, storing its solution in a table for later use.","permalink":"/concepts/algorithm-design-paradigms/dynamic-programming"},{"id":"algorithm-design-paradigms/greedy-algorithms","title":"Greedy Algorithms","description":"Many problems ask not just for a solution, but for the best one they build the solution step by step and, at each step, they take the choice that looks best right now, without ever reconsidering it.","permalink":"/concepts/algorithm-design-paradigms/greedy-algorithms"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);